  <h1>What Does a CP12 Landlord Certificate Cover?</h1>
  <p>If you rent out a property with gas appliances in England or Wales, this isn't optional — it's a legal requirement. Here's what it actually involves.</p>

  <h2>What CP12 means</h2>
  <p>CP12 is the industry name for the Landlord Gas Safety Record — the certificate a Gas Safe registered engineer issues after checking every gas appliance, flue and fitting in a rented property.</p>

  <h2>What gets checked</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>All gas appliances the landlord owns (boiler, gas fire, gas hob, gas oven)</li>
    <li>Flues and ventilation</li>
    <li>Gas pressure and safety devices</li>
    <li>Signs of leaks, corrosion, or unsafe installation</li>
    <li>Carbon monoxide alarm presence and function, where applicable</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Appliances the <em>tenant</em> owns and brings with them aren't the landlord's legal responsibility to certify — but it's still worth knowing what's in the property.</p>

  <h2>Your legal obligations as a landlord</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A gas safety check every 12 months, by a Gas Safe registered engineer</li>
    <li>A copy of the current certificate given to tenants within 28 days of the check (or before they move in, for new tenancies)</li>
    <li>Records kept for at least 2 years</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What happens if you don't</h2>
  <p>This isn't a "might get a strongly worded letter" situation — landlords can face prosecution and unlimited fines for failing to meet gas safety obligations, and it can seriously complicate things if there's ever an incident or an insurance claim.</p>

  <h2>Multiple properties?</h2>
  <p>If you're managing more than one rental, we can help coordinate scheduling and renewal reminders so certificates don't quietly lapse between tenancies. <a href="/gas-safety-landlords/">See our Landlord Gas Safety page →</a></p>
